{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB08909 - MONDO_9376",
  "verbalization": "Glycerol Phenylbutyrate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Carbamoyl Phosphate Synthetase I Deficiency Disease through a series of interconnected pathways. The drug is associated with Argininosuccinic Aciduria and Urea Cycle Disorder, which are both linked to Carbamoyl Phosphate Synthetase I Deficiency Disease. These conditions are characterized by impaired urea synthesis, leading to the accumulation of toxic byproducts such as ammonia and phenylbutyric acid. Glycerol Phenylbutyrate is known to inhibit the urea cycle by interfering with the enzyme responsible for converting argininosuccinic acid to citrulline, thereby exacerbating the accumulation of phenylbutyric acid and ammonia. This mechanism is supported by multiple evidence paths, which collectively suggest that the drug's effects on the urea cycle contribute to the development of the disease. The consistent association across different pathways highlights a plausible mechanistic link, though the exact role of Glycerol Phenylbutyrate in the disease process remains to be fully elucidated.",
